{"id":1873,"date":"2020-12-09T18:54:34","date_gmt":"2020-12-10T02:54:34","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1873"},"modified":"2024-06-03T13:46:06","modified_gmt":"2024-06-03T20:46:06","slug":"zumi-python-how-to-use-jupyter","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-how-to-use-jupyter\/","title":{"rendered":"0.2: How to Use Jupyter"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150aa206a\" name=\"6a22150aa206a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is Jupyter Notebook?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Jupyter Notebook is an environment where you can write and run code. You can also include descriptive text (like what you&#8217;re reading right now!) to describe your code. Many researchers use it to write up their reports with embedded code examples that you can run and test for yourself. In the following lessons you will be using the Python programming language. However, Jupyter Notebook supports over 40 programming languages![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1883&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150aa2165\" name=\"6a22150aa2165\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Opening a Jupyter Notebook<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you first open Jupyter, you will see a screen similar to the one below. It shows you a list of all your current notebooks. The picture shows the different parts of the home screen. To open a notebook, click on the name of the notebook you would like to open. The color of the notebook icon will indicate which notebooks are already open.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1886&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150aa21b6\" name=\"6a22150aa21b6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Inside a Jupyter Notebook<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You write the code for your Zumi inside Jupyter Notebook. When you open a lesson, you should already see cells filled with text and code. To go through the lesson, you will need to run code, stop code, and save your changes! The most important tools that you will use are highlighted for you below:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1890&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150aa22fa\" name=\"6a22150aa22fa\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Cells<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-size: medium;\">A typical Jupyter notebook is divided into cells. Some cells have text, such as the one you are currently reading, and some cells can run code.<\/span><\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Running-code\">Running code<\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">The cell below is an example of a code cell. To run the cell and execute code, select it so that it is highlighted in\u00a0blue\u00a0or\u00a0green. For future reference, a cell outline in blue is selected but not in edit mode. A cell outlined is green is selected and in edit mode. In either state, you can click &#8220;Run&#8221; in the toolbar or press\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">Shift<\/span>\u00a0+\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">Enter<\/span>.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">In [ ]: print(\"This is a code cell!\")<\/pre>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<div class=\"cell text_cell unselected rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render rendered_html\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">After you run a cell, the output of the code will appear below it. The number next to the cell will also change from\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">In[]<\/span>\u00a0to\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">In[1]<\/span>. The number indicates the order that the cells executed and completed their processes. In Jupyter Notebook, you can run either all cells or sections of your code at a time! Try running the above code again and it will change from\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">In[1]<\/span>\u00a0to\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">In[2]<\/span>.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">If the\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">In[]<\/span>\u00a0indicator does not appear next to a cell, that cell\u00a0<strong>cannot<\/strong> run code. For example, try &#8220;running&#8221; the cell below.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"cell text_cell unselected rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render rendered_html\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">print(\"This is not a code cell\")<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"cell text_cell unselected rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render rendered_html\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">This cell was formatted into text instead. These cells are called\u00a0<strong>markdown<\/strong>\u00a0cells. Sometimes you can switch your cell into a different type by accident. If this happens, go to &#8220;Cell&#8221; then &#8220;Cell Type&#8221; in the toolbar to switch between the two. Try changing the cell above from markdown to code.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Stopping-the-code\">Stopping the code<\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Sometimes you need to stop the code that is currently running. Maybe you have a while loop that is going forever and you are trying to escape! Next to the &#8220;Run&#8221; button in the toolbar you will find a black square. This is the stop button and will terminate the cell that is running. Run the cell below (which runs forever) and then stop the code.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">count=0\r\nwhile True:\r\n    print(count)\r\n    count+=1<\/pre>\n<p>Did you notice while the cell is running there&#8217;s an\u00a0<strong>asterisk<\/strong>\u00a0next to the cell? It looks like this:\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">In[*]<\/span>. Any cell with an asterisk is still running. Avoid trying to run more cells if one is still running.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">count=0\r\nwhile True:\r\n    print(count)\r\n    count+=1<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Adding-cells\">Adding cells<a class=\"anchor-link\" href=\"http:\/\/zumidashboard.ai:5555\/notebooks\/Zumi_Content_en\/Lesson\/0.2%20How%20to%20Use%20Jupyter.ipynb#Adding-cells\">\u00b6<\/a><\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">If you want to create a new cell, go to the toolbar and click &#8220;Insert&#8221;. In the menu you have options to insert a cell above or below the current cell.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1893&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Deleting-cells\">Deleting cells<a class=\"anchor-link\" href=\"http:\/\/zumidashboard.ai:5555\/notebooks\/Zumi_Content_en\/Lesson\/0.2%20How%20to%20Use%20Jupyter.ipynb#Deleting-cells\">\u00b6<\/a><\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Under &#8220;Edit&#8221; in the toolbar, you will find many tools including &#8220;Delete Cells&#8221;. This will delete any cells that are currently selected.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1894&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<h3 id=\"Locked-cells\">Locked cells<\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Some cells that you see on tutorials (including this one) are locked. This means that you can&#8217;t delete or edit them. Try editing the different cells below. You shouldn&#8217;t be able to edit some of them.<\/span><\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<h1>I can be edited and deleted<\/h1>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<h1>I can be edited, but I can&#8217;t be deleted<\/h1>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<h1>I can&#8217;t be edited or deleted<\/h1>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150aa23ad\" name=\"6a22150aa23ad\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Notebook Functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Restarting-a-notebook\">Restarting a notebook<a class=\"anchor-link\" href=\"http:\/\/zumidashboard.ai:5555\/notebooks\/Zumi_Content_en\/Lesson\/0.2%20How%20to%20Use%20Jupyter.ipynb#Restarting-a-notebook\">\u00b6<\/a><\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">There may be times when you want to clear all of the output from your cells and restart all of your code. In the toolbar there is a tab called &#8220;Kernel&#8221;, and in its menu you will see an option to &#8220;Restart &amp; Clear Output&#8221;. Once you select it, all of the output will be erased and those cells will be ready to be run again! You can double check by looking for empty\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">In[]<\/span>\u00a0brackets.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1896&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Shutting-down-a-Jupyter-Notebook\">Shutting down a Jupyter Notebook<\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Even if you close the tab, a notebook will remain open until shutdown. It is recommended you leave only one notebook open at a time. To shut down a notebook, select it and click on the orange &#8220;Shutdown&#8221; button. Be careful not to delete it by accident!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1897&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 6 What is Jupyter Notebook? [vc_column_text]Jupyter Notebook is an environment where you can write and run code. You can also include descriptive text (like what you're reading right&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-how-to-use-jupyter\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2051,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":810,"_lesson_preview":""},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2177,2